WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.020
Our goal is the order restriction.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:06.270
If a real circulant Hadamard
matrix has order greater than one,

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:11.400
then its order must be four times the
square of an odd positive integer. We will

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.680
prove the square and parity restrictions,
then use the deeper oddness result.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.940
We saw that we can encode the
first row by a polynomial.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.920
For a row of signs, write h of X as
the sum of h sub j times X to the j,

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.870
with every coefficient
positive or negative one.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:34.440
Reverse the exponents to form h star.The

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.180
Hadamard condition gives h times
h star equals n in the group ring,

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:42.030
where X to the n equals one.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:45.580
Now choose X equal to one.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:49.430
Reversing the powers does
not change the value at one:

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.780
every power of one, including
a negative power, is still one.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.970
Both polynomials therefore become
the same integer sum, h of one.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:03.900
Substituting into the identity
gives n equal to h of one times

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.600
h star of one, which is h of one squared.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.840
Since h of one is an integer,
n is a perfect square.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.840
We will now show that n must be even.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.200
Because n is greater than
one, take two different rows.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:26.040
Their dot product is zero. Each term
is positive one or negative one.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.840
Pair every positive contribution
with a negative contribution.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:33.320
For the sum to vanish,
their counts must agree.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:39.700
There are n terms altogether, so n is
even. We now have an even perfect square.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:46.040
Its positive square root must be even too,
because an odd integer has an odd square.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.073
Write that square root as two u.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.800
Then n is the square of two
u, which is four u squared.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.680
There is one more
restriction: u must be odd.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.080
This uses a deeper argument
with the polynomial identity.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:07.300
We accept that result here; the square and
parity arguments alone do not prove it.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.400
Combining these inputs gives
n equal to four u squared,

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:12.800
with u an odd positive integer.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:17.550
The case u equal to one gives
our known example of order four.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.860
It remains to rule out u greater than
one. We proceed by contradiction.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:27.320
Assume a circulant Hadamard matrix
exists of order four u squared,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.180
with u odd and greater than one.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:35.687
The smallest remaining value is u equal
to three, giving order thirty six.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.460
This will guide the remaining argument,
while the proof must cover every odd u

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:44.160
greater than one. Order one
remains the trivial case.
